Why This Is Asked

Tests practical ability to design a scalable, tenant-aware data intake: dynamic schemas, low-latency validation, and reliable processing.

Key Concepts

- Event-driven ingestion with per-tenant schema discovery
- Caching and TTL for schema lookups
- Fast validation library (e.g., Ajv)
- Idempotent writes and deduplication
- Dead-letter queue for bad data
- Schema evolution testing and compatibility checks

Code Example

```javascript
// Ingestion function (Node.js)
const ajv = new Ajv();
let schemaCache = new Map();
async function getSchemaForTenant(tenantId){
  if(schemaCache.has(tenantId)) return schemaCache.get(tenantId);
  const cfg = await AppConfigClient.getConfigurationSetting({key: `schema:${tenantId}`});
  const schema = JSON.parse(cfg.value);
  schemaCache.set(tenantId, schema);
  setTimeout(() => schemaCache.delete(tenantId), 60_000);
  return schema;
}
async function validateAndRoute(msg){
  const tenantId = msg.tenantId;
  const schema = await getSchemaForTenant(tenantId);
  const validate = ajv.compile(schema);
  const valid = validate(msg.payload);
  if(!valid){ await DLQ.push({tenantId, msg, errors: validate.errors}); return; }
  await CosmosDB.upsert('events', tenantId, msg.payload, {dedupId: msg.id});
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you test schema evolution across tenants and ensure backward compatibility?
- How would you handle schema removal or deprecation without breaking in-flight events?

Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a small, reliable serverless API with per-tenant isolation, idempotent writes, and TTL in Cosmos DB. It also checks basic validation, simple retries, and test strategy.

Key Concepts

- Azure Functions HTTP trigger and route parameters
- Cosmos DB partitioning by tenantId and idempotent upserts
- JSON payload validation against a minimal schema
- TTL retention in Cosmos DB containers
- Basic retry/backoff and error handling
- Unit and end-to-end testing with local emulation

Code Example

```javascript
// Example outline for the HTTP POST handler
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  const tenantId = req.params.tenantId;
  const body = req.body;
  // basic validation: eventId required, payload present
  if (!tenantId || !body || !body.eventId) {
    context.res = { status: 400, body: 'Invalid payload' };
    return;
  }
  const item = {
    id: body.eventId,
    tenantId,
    payload: body,
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString()
  };
  // upsert to Cosmos DB (idempotent by using eventId as id)
  await cosmosClient.database(DATABASE).container(CONTAINER)
    .items.upsert(item);
  context.res = { status: 200, body: { ok: true } };
};
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this with a local Cosmos DB emulator and simulate retries?
- How would you evolve the schema when new fields are introduced while preserving backward compatibility?

